<p>
  I’ve been a senior UX designer at Red Hat since 2021, based in Vermont and working on enterprise
  digital platforms—first redhat.com, now primarily docs.redhat.com. The work is systems-heavy: big
  catalogs, embedded engineers, content strategists, and surfaces that have to hold up for years.
</p>

<p>Two chapters so far:</p>

<h2>redhat.com — homepage, navigation, and research</h2>
<p>
  My first couple of years focused on redhat.com—simplifying a homepage that had been featuring dozens
  of products, redesigning the global product navigation away from a “wall of products,” refreshing
  resource page templates, and co-leading Summit research with customers in the room.
</p>
<p><a href="/work-preview/red-hat/redhat-com/">Read the case study →</a></p>

<h2>Unified Learn — documentation and learning</h2>
<p>
  Since then I’ve led design on docs.redhat.com (~55k weekday visitors). The platform started as
  product documentation and has grown into <strong>Unified Learn</strong>—documentation, learning
  paths, tutorials, and more under one URL while docs.redhat.com remains the address customers know.
</p>
